 get  to this whole time because now we know  that commit to 54 e whatever whatever is  the one that introduced the change in  the first place that our bug fix is now  correcting so we can label commit to 54  e whatever as a 1 this is a commit that  introduced a bug so that whole process  is kind of involved you definitely don't  wanna do it by hand but fortunately it's  very easy to automate that with a bunch  of pie  fun in particular you can use sub  process check output and then just pass  in your get commands as a string and  that will get you back the response as a  string you do a little Python string  processing some regular expressions and  whatnot and you can repeat that whole  process for the entire commit history of  your project of your whole repository so  you can go through for every commit in  your history and label it a 1 or a 0 by  whether or not it introduced a bug ok  that was step one but that was the hard  step so from here on out is smoother  sailing step two we want to build  features for each of those commits so we  have the labels now we need to build the  features and in this case get log is  going to be our workhorse in particular  get log with the dash dash stat option  because that gives you an output that  looks like this over here and right off  the bat there are a couple obvious  choices or a couple obvious things you  can use as features so for example how  many files did this commit change that  might be an interesting feature  how many insertions how many new lines  of code to this edge how many deletions  how many lines did this take out with a  little bit of creativity you can also  think of for example how long is the  commit message if you tag your commit  messages you could build a categorical  feature on whether it's an enhancement  or a bug fix or a refactor or something  like that did this work happen on the  weekend did this work happen after 11:00  p.m. all these kind of things you could  make and if you really want to make  friends you could build a commit a  commit or a feature on who made it but  use at your own risk  so anyway step 2 got our features step 3  again it's the easy one in this case we  used a random forest classifier built a  model on these features and labels and  it kind of works so we get an AUC of  0.76 which is really not that bad we can  look at some of the feature importances  in just a simple simple model length of  the commit message shows up on top for  most predictive  additions deletions time actually does  turn out to be kind of predictive here  so yeah this once you've done all the  work to build up your labels and build  up your features building the model kind  of works but here I'm gonna rain on the  parade a little bit and repeat the  caveat that this really absolutely  requires that you have really good  commit hygiene so the example I'm  showing here is from running this on  kind of our most pristine internal  repository and trying it on things where  this the coding standard and the process  standard is a little bit lower really  degrades a performance one in terms of  model and two in terms of how much you  should trust the model like being able  to duplicate the labels if the labels  aren't meaningful isn't particularly  useful so big warning this really really  requires good commit hygiene and then  the last step is to hook this up so that  it runs automatically every time you  make a new commit and to do this we're  going to use one last little bit of git  which is called git hooks so git hooks  are scripts that run automatically every  time you take a particular get action so  for example you could have a pre-commit  hook that runs before like you're trying  to make a new kit new commit but before  we get saved before it gets recorded  this pre-commit hook script will run  there's another one for commit message  which every time you try to save a  commit message that hook will run and  but one that we care about is the post  commit hook so every time you make a new  commit and record it this post commit  hook script will run so what you can do  is make a simple little Python script  put it in your project dot git slash  hooks and a file called post commit and  this file will run every time you make a  new commit so what you can do is pull in  a few packages check the output of your  latest get log call the - - one means  only tell me about the most recent one  commit do whatever feature parsing you  did in the first place to generate your  model load up your pickled model and  predict on the features of your most  so big drumroll the final output of all  this is something that looks like this  you make a new commit and you get a  little thing that says this is how risky  this particular commit is so that's kind  of the the bulk of the talk that that  concludes the modeling portion but  before I wrap up and open up for  questions I want to quickly run through  a couple things you can do on your own  to improve your commit hygiene not just  because you might want to do this kind  of modeling exercise but because it's  useful in general so I say it's useful  in general why is that the case well  number one clean commit histories can  help you understand the evolution of a  codebase say you go on vacation for two  weeks you come back you want to see what  happened if your colleagues use good  commit hygiene you can just pull up the  repository see the messages see what's  been changed see what's been worked on  at a glance see what's going on in the  codebase that's very handy number two  maybe even more handy if you have good  commit hygiene every single line of code  in your codebase is documented because  if you come up on a block of code that  you don't understand and there's no  inline comment you can pull up you can  like get blamed see what commits  introduced that get logs go find those  commits go find every commit that touch  those lines and see what introduced it  how did it get changed why di